# The same match as match.case, with player one scripted after "Go!":
# walk right for a second, jump, attack. Scores the dynamics -- walk speed,
# jump arc, animation timing -- not just the settled frame. Dolphin runs the
# match.case menu route, then the same three inputs in dump frames
# (port frame + 1515: the timers put the console match start at dump
# 1648 and the port at frame 133). The port holds the stick with an explicit hold count
# because its stick tokens auto-release after four frames.
description: VS match with P1 walking, jumping and attacking (Onett)
run_frames: 340
ref_frames: 2400
align: search
window: 8
ref_lead: -1515
env: MELEE_BOOT_MODE=14
env: MELEE_BOOT_MATCH=13,12,9,0,0
env: MELEE_BOOT_STOCKS=3
input: 145: right*60
input: 235: x
input: 275: a
ref_input: 236:+START
ref_input: 240:-START
ref_input: 400:+START
ref_input: 404:-START
ref_input: 760:MAIN:0.5:0.0
ref_input: 768:MAIN:0.5:0.5
ref_input: 820:+A
ref_input: 824:-A
ref_input: 950:+A
ref_input: 954:-A
ref_input: 1000:P2:+A
ref_input: 1004:P2:-A
ref_input: 1060:MAIN:0.5:1.0
ref_input: 1120:MAIN:0.5:0.5
ref_input: 1130:MAIN:1.0:0.5
ref_input: 1140:MAIN:0.5:0.5
ref_input: 1150:MAIN:0.5:0.0
ref_input: 1157:MAIN:0.5:0.5
ref_input: 1170:+A
ref_input: 1174:-A
ref_input: 1180:P2:MAIN:0.5:1.0
ref_input: 1240:P2:MAIN:0.5:0.5
ref_input: 1250:P2:MAIN:1.0:0.5
ref_input: 1260:P2:MAIN:0.5:0.5
ref_input: 1270:P2:MAIN:0.5:0.0
ref_input: 1277:P2:MAIN:0.5:0.5
ref_input: 1290:P2:+A
ref_input: 1294:P2:-A
ref_input: 1340:+START
ref_input: 1344:-START
ref_input: 1420:MAIN:0.5:1.0
ref_input: 1452:MAIN:0.5:0.5
ref_input: 1480:+A
ref_input: 1484:-A
ref_input: 1660:MAIN:1.0:0.5
ref_input: 1720:MAIN:0.5:0.5
ref_input: 1750:+X
ref_input: 1754:-X
ref_input: 1790:+A
ref_input: 1794:-A
checks: 195, 251, 315

# For tools/pc_divergence.py this is the case that matters: the same three
# inputs go to both sides and every frame afterwards is compared, so a walk
# that accelerates differently, a jump with the wrong arc or an attack whose
# animation runs at the wrong rate all show up as a frame number rather than
# as a feeling about a video. See match.case for why mode and scene differ by
# construction.
ignore: mode, scene
# Measured, not guessed: the port's match runs three frames ahead of the
# console's, and the harness found it by searching shifts (+3 buys 113 more
# frames of agreement). It is not input latency -- under the shift the spawn
# fall matches from its first frame as well -- but the entry into the match:
# the port boots the debug-VS scene while Dolphin walks into a normal VS
# match, and the two start counting match frames from slightly different
# places.
#
# Declaring it here is what lets this case measure what it is for. With the
# offset taken out, the action-state sequence is identical on both sides and
# the residual is a real one: the fall off the spawn platform is bit-identical
# frame by frame down to y = -0.306, and then the console lands (y snaps to
# 0.0001, state Landing) while the port keeps falling for two more frames to
# y = -4.106 before it notices. The port's ground collision is two frames
# late. match_dash.case reproduces it with the same numbers.
ref_shift: 3
trace_frames: 1150
ref_trace_frames: 3100
